-- salle_makeen.fleet -- the dining room at Makeen, judged as one thing.
--
-- Every check this file makes is one no single machine can fail. The box
-- is a faultless machine on its own; so is a second box that also calls
-- itself the server of `makeen`. Together they are a network where two
-- machines hand out the same addresses and neither one is wrong. That is
-- what a fleet is for: the coherence of a SET, declared where the set is.
--
-- LINK says these machines share a wire, which is what makes the address
-- checks mean anything. Without it a fleet is an estate and not a
-- network, and only the identity checks apply.
--
-- HARDWARE says which physical unit each member is, which is what lets
-- the court check the promise against the machine: a device that asks on
-- this link and is in nobody's register would never get an address, and
-- there is no pool to fall back on. Before HDW-1 that correspondence
-- lived in a `-device ...,mac=` flag inside experiment/os6_names.sh --
-- the one fact about this deployment that was not declared anywhere.
--
-- No member carries a KEY yet, and the court does not refuse that: a
-- device's key is made on the device, on its first boot, from its own
-- randomness, and a declaration written beforehand cannot know it.
-- `stzos fleet` reports who is unenrolled rather than pretending
-- otherwise. `machines/fleet_temoin.machine` and
-- `experiment/os7_fleet.sh` show the whole arc on a real device: a key
-- made, published, enrolled, and a record verified by somebody who does
-- not hold the secret (FLT-1, 2026-09-14).

DEFINE FLEET salle_makeen AS (
  LINK salle
) RATIONALE "One room, one wire: the box that is the network's address and everything that asks it for one"

DEFINE MEMBER boitier AS (
  DECLARATION "makeen_names.machine",
  HARDWARE "52:54:00:12:34:01"
) RATIONALE "The box. It serves the link, so the fleet lets exactly one member do that and this is the one"

DEFINE MEMBER caisse AS (
  DECLARATION "caisse_makeen.machine",
  HARDWARE "52:54:00:12:34:61"
) RATIONALE "The till. It declares no address at all and is told one, and the fleet can now say WHICH device will be told: this hardware is the one makeen_names promises 192.168.10.40 to, so the promise and the machine are held against each other rather than hoped about"
